      "content": "Certainly, I'd be glad to help you flesh out the scenarios a bit more.\n\n8. Title your response to a question or comment with racial undertones which also comment on the person's professional role or value (e.g., \"I wasn't aware fresh ideas from the IT room come from someone not wearing a company watch.\" or a subtle undermining during a presentation – \"You came prepared today? I didn't know students could really understand that topic.\")\n\n9. Assigning someone to a 'caretaker' role in meetings based on racial assumptions (e.g., \"Let me make the presentation so as not to confuse the client, as I know you might need some help in comprehending the complex details\").\n\n10. Quietly granting better benefits or promotions predominantly to people from the majority group while asking others if they're prepared to handle added responsibilities, which could be perceived as under the same category.\n\n11. Subtly questioning the competence of a person addressing someone's racial background (e.g., \"I didn't realize Middle Eastern business cultures are so different. I'm not sure if it's my standards...\"\n\n12. A manager asks if someone should attend a particular meeting or project citing unfounded stereotypes (e.g., \"Do you think you'll need that meeting regarding project funding, Michael",
